That’s an interesting inversion.

A story is often an outcome-first compression. It tells you what happened while leaving you to reconstruct how it happened. The reader mentally fills in the causal machinery.

The opposite is a process-first generator. It specifies transformations and lets the outcome emerge.

Different fields give that inversion different names:

* Algorithm — specify the process; the output is whatever the process computes.
* Recipe — specify actions; the meal emerges.
* Proof — specify logical steps; the theorem follows.
* Simulation — specify rules; behavior emerges.
* Grammar — specify production rules; sentences emerge.
* Game mechanics — specify constraints and actions; stories emerge through play.

So, if a story is “outcome → infer process,” then an algorithm is roughly “process → infer outcome.”

⸻

Your dragonfly metaphor fits this well.

Dragonflies don’t compute every future trajectory exhaustively. They exploit invariants. Their visual system predicts where interception will occur and continually updates the estimate. They aren’t chasing where the prey is; they’re converging on where the shared dynamics require it to be.

Questions can work the same way.

Instead of saying:

“Do X.”

you ask:

“What would happen if X?”

The listener begins simulating X themselves. Their own reasoning performs part of the computation.

Likewise,

“Why is this true?”

isn’t only a request for information. It can be an instruction:

“Build a causal model.”

The form is interrogative, but the function is generative.

⸻

This is why your observation about using questions as statements and commands as questions is useful.

There are at least three layers:

Surface form	Hidden function
Question	Request, command, invitation to simulate
Statement	Observation, hypothesis, challenge
Command	Suggestion, question about willingness, experiment

Human communication routinely decouples syntax from function.

A parent saying,

“Is your room clean?”

rarely seeks information. The intended function is closer to:

“Please clean your room.”

Conversely,

“Try looking from the other side.”

may be grammatically imperative but function as:

“Would a different perspective help?”

⸻

This suggests another axis beyond genre: speech act versus surface syntax.

You can think of a message as having at least four independent components:

* Form — what it looks like.
* Function — what cognitive operation it tries to induce.
* Semantics — what it refers to.
* Pragmatics — why it is being said now.

Your conversations often separate these intentionally.

A metaphor may function as an algorithm.

A joke may function as a probe.

A question may function as a compiler directive.

A story may function as compressed documentation.

⸻

The interesting implication is that language becomes less like a sequence of sentences and more like a way of steering another mind’s computation.

Sometimes the fastest way to communicate isn’t to provide the answer. It’s to choose a form that causes the other person to perform exactly the reasoning you wanted them to perform. That can make the resulting understanding more durable because they participated in constructing it rather than only receiving it.